   672  
   673  ICONT(1)            UNIX Programmer's Manual             ICONT(1)
   674  
   675  
   676  NAME
   677       icont - process Icon programs
   678  
   679  SYNOPSIS
   680       icont [ option ... ] file ... [ -x arg ... ]
   681  
   682  DESCRIPTION
   683       The program icont is a command processor for running Version
   684       8 Icon programs.  Used in its simplest form, it produces a
   685       file suitable for interpretation by the Icon interpreter.
   686       Processing consists of two phases: translation and linking.
   687       During translation, each Icon source file is translated into
   688       an intermediate language called ucode; during linking, the
   689       one or more ucode files are combined and a single icode file
   690       is produced.  Unless the -o option is specified, the name of
   691       the resulting icode file is formed by deleting the suffix of
   692       the first input file named on the command line.  If the -x
   693       argument is used, the file is automatically executed by the
   694       interpreter and any arguments following the -x are passed as
   695       execution arguments to the Icon program itself.
   696  
   697       Files whose names end in .icn are assumed to be Icon source
   698       programs.  The .icn suffix may be omitted; it will be sup-
   699       plied automatically.  These programs are translated, and the
   700       intermediate code is left in two ucode files of the same
   701       name with .u1 and .u2 substituted for .icn.  The ucode files
   702       normally are deleted when icont completes.  Files whose
   703       names end in .u1 are assumed to refer to ucode files from a
   704       previous translation; these files and the corresponding .u2
   705       files are included in the linking phase after any .icn files
   706       have been translated.  The suffix .u can be used in place of
   707       .u1; in this case the 1 is supplied automatically.  A .u1 or
   708       .u2 file that is explicitly named is not deleted.  Icon
   709       source programs may be read from standard input.  The argu-
   710       ment - signifies the use of standard input as a source file.
   711       In this case, the ucode files are named stdin.u1 and
   712       stdin.u2 and the icode file is named stdin.
   713  
   714       The following options are recognized by icont:
   715  
   716       -c  Suppress the linking phase.  The ucode code files are
   717           not deleted.
   718  
   719       -m  Preprocess each .icn source file with the m4(1) macro
   720           processor before translation.
   721  
   722       -o output
   723           Name the icode file output.
   724  
   725       -s  Suppress informative messages from the translator and
   726           linker.  Normally, both informative messages and error
   727           messages are sent to standard error output.
   728  
   729       -t  Arrange for &trace to have an initial value of -1 when
   730  
   731  
   732  Printed 12/29/89Icon Project - 1/1/1990 - IPD109                1
   733  
   734  
   735  
   736  
   737  
   738  
   739  ICONT(1)            UNIX Programmer's Manual             ICONT(1)
   740  
   741  
   742           the program is executed.  Normally, &trace has an ini-
   743           tial value of 0.
   744  
   745       -u  Issue warning messages for undeclared identifiers in the
   746           program.  The warnings are issued during the linking
   747           phase.
   748  
   749       Icon has several tables related to the translation and link-
   750       ing of programs.  These tables are large enough for most
   751       programs, but their sizes can be changed, if necessary, by
   752       the -S option. This option has the form -S[cfgilnrstCFL]n,
   753       where the letter following the S specifies the table and n
   754       is the number of storage units to allocate for the table.
   755       The tables and their default sizes are:
   756  
   757            c   constant table             100
   758            f   field table                100
   759            g   global symbol table        200
   760            i   identifier table           500
   761            l   local symbol table         100
   762            n   line number space         1000
   763            r   field table for records    100
   764            s   string space             20000
   765            t   tree space               15000
   766            C   code buffer              15000
   767            F   file names                  10
   768            L   labels                     500
   769  
   770       The units depend on the table involved, but the default
   771       values can be used as a general guide for appropriate set-
   772       tings of -S options without knowing the units.
   773  
   774       The environment variable IPATH controls the location of
   775       files specified in link directives. The value of IPATH
   776       should be a blank-separated string of the form p1 p2 ...  pn
   777       where the pi name directories.  Each directory is searched
   778       in turn to locate files named in link directives. The
   779       default value for IPATH is . , that is, the current direc-
   780       tory.  The current path is always searched first, regardless
   781       of the value of IPATH.
   782  
   783       The icode file produced by the Icon linker is executable.
   784       For example, the command
   785  
   786            icont hello.icn
   787  
   788       produces a file named hello that can be run by the command
   789  
   790            hello
   791  
   792  
   793       Arguments can be passed to the Icon program by following the
   794       program name with the arguments.  Any such arguments are
   795       passed to the main procedure as a list of strings.
   796  
   797  
   798  Printed 12/29/89Icon Project - 1/1/1990 - IPD109                2
   799  
   800  
   801  
   802  
   803  
   804  
   805  ICONT(1)            UNIX Programmer's Manual             ICONT(1)
   806  
   807  
   808       The location of iconx, the executor for icode files, is
   809       built into an icode file when it is produced. This location
   810       can be overridden by setting the environment variable ICONX
   811       as described below.  If ICONX is not set and iconx is not
   812       found on the built-in path, PATH is searched for it.
   813  
   814       When an Icon program is executed, several environment vari-
   815       ables are examined to determine certain execution parame-
   816       ters.  Expect for ICONX, NOERRBUF, and ICONCORE, the values
   817       assigned to these variables should be numbers.  The vari-
   818       ables that affect execution and the interpretations of their
   819       values follow. Numbers in parentheses are the default
   820       values.
   821  
   822       ICONX
   823           If this environment variable is set, it specifies the
   824           location of iconx to use to execute an icode file.
   825  
   826       TRACE
   827           Initialize the value of &trace.  If this variable has a
   828           value, it overrides the translation-time -t option.
   829  
   830       NOERRBUF
   831           By default, &errout is buffered.  If this variable is
   832           set, &errout is not buffered.
   833  
   834       ICONCORE
   835           If set, a core dump is produced for error termination.
   836  
   837       STRSIZE (65000)
   838           The initial size of the string space, in bytes.  The
   839           string space grows if necessary, but it never shrinks.
   840  
   841       HEAPSIZE (65000)
   842           The initial size of the allocated block region, in
   843           bytes.  The block region grows if necessary, but it
   844           never shrinks.
   845  
   846       COEXPSIZE (2000)
   847           The size, in words, of each co-expression block.
   848  
   849       MSTKSIZE (10000)
   850           The size, in words, of the main interpreter stack.
   851  
   852       STATSIZE (20480)
   853           The size, in bytes, of the static region in which co-
   854           expression blocks are allocated. If co-expressions are
   855           not implemented, the default size is 1024.
   856  
   857       STATINCR
   858           The size of the increment used when the static region is
   859           expanded.  The default increment is one-fourth of the
   860           initial size of the static region.
   861  
   862  
   863  
   864  Printed 12/29/89Icon Project - 1/1/1990 - IPD109                3
   865  
   866  
   867  
   868  
   869  
   870  
   871  ICONT(1)            UNIX Programmer's Manual             ICONT(1)
   872  
   873  
   874       QLSIZE (5000)
   875           The size, in bytes, of the region used for pointers to
   876           strings during garbage collection (fixed-regions imple-
   877           mentations only).
   878  
   879       MEMMON
   880           The name of the output file for memory monitoring.
   881  
   882  FILES
   883       icont     Icon command processor
   884       iconx     Icon executor
